---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// No hero photograph. The template's artwork is a placeholder and the obvious
// replacement — a stock lecture theatre, or something generated — would say
// nothing this course is about. The arithmetic below is the argument, so it
// gets the space instead.
//
// 130 hours is one 6-unit course. Attempts = 130h / (time to failure + time to
// retry), rounded to something honest rather than something precise.
const loops = [
  { what: "A Souls boss", fail: "90 s", retry: "15 s", attempts: "~4,500" },
  { what: "A ranked match", fail: "30 min", retry: "1 min", attempts: "~250" },
  { what: "A weekly quiz", fail: "1 week", retry: "1 week", attempts: "12" },
  { what: "An assignment", fail: "3 weeks", retry: "never", attempts: "3" },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>The whole course in one table</h2>

  <p>
    A six-unit course is about 130 hours. So is a Souls game. Nobody finishes
    one of those on willpower, and the difference is not that games are fun —
    it is how long you wait to find out you were wrong.
  </p>

  <div class="loop-table-wrap">
    <table class="loop-table">
      <caption>
        Correction opportunities bought by 130 hours, at four loop lengths
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Loop</th>
          <th scope="col">To failure</th>
          <th scope="col">To retry</th>
          <th scope="col">Attempts in 130 h</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          loops.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{row.what}</th>
              <td class="num">{row.fail}</td>
              <td class="num">{row.retry}</td>
              <td class="num attempts">{row.attempts}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="pull">
    Same hours. Same person. Fifteen hundred times the chances to correct.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>

  <p>
    Weeks 2&ndash;5 are the MOBA half: three clocks, a win condition per
    contest, and a rating that survives between them. You take a thirty-minute
    sitting and give every minute of it a job, first on paper and then in
    whatever you can build.
  </p>

  <p>
    Weeks 6&ndash;9 are the Souls half. A hundred hours of content becomes
    finishable when it becomes nine fights that each end &mdash; and that is a
    capacity no rating system can give you, however well you build it. Working
    out why is the argument the course turns on.
  </p>

  <p>
    Weeks 10&ndash;12 you point both at a subject you actively dislike, cover a
    quarter of it in about thirty hours, and sit an exam we set on the part you
    chose. Everything else you hand in is something you wrote about yourself.
    That one isn&rsquo;t.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>

  <p>
    Anyone who has watched a semester go past without being able to say what
    they did with it. There are no prerequisites and the reading is light, but
    this is not a soft course: you will play two demanding games attentively,
    ship a mechanism that survives you being tired, and then teach yourself
    something you have already failed to start.
  </p>

  <p>
    You do not need to write code. Two of the three assessments offer a
    paper route and a built route, marked identically, and a paper system in
    daily use beats an unfinished program every time.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, and the argument each one is responsible for.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>
        Two of them, in weeks 3 and 7, and both are spent playing. That is not
        a joke.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Two live demonstrations and one run at something you can&rsquo;t stand.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>
        Including why a different exam for every student is marked the way it
        is.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* Narrow viewports get a scroller rather than a squeezed four-column table;
     the numeric columns stop being comparable the moment they wrap. */
  .loop-table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-block: 1.5rem;
  }

  .loop-table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 30rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  .loop-table caption {
    caption-side: bottom;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--at-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .loop-table th,
  .loop-table td {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .loop-table thead th {
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .loop-table tbody th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .loop-table .num {
    font-family: var(--at-font-mono);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* The rightmost column is the punchline, so it is the only thing on the
     table wearing the brand colour. */
  .loop-table .attempts {
    color: var(--at-primary);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .pull {
    margin-block: 1.5rem 2.5rem;
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    color: var(--at-heading);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>
